speechtext:
The committee were ten in number. As I say, the selection was not made upon the basis of representing countries at all. The council of the league selected these gentlemen because it considered them to be eminently qualified. If this be of interest, I might perhaps give the full list of the gentlemen composing the committee. They were:
M. Mineichiro Adatci, Envoy Extraordinary and Minister Plenipotentiary of His Majesty the Emperor of Japan, at Brussels;
M. Rafael Altamira, Senator, Professor of the Faculty of Law of the University of Madrid;
M. Clovis Bevilaqua, Legal Adviser to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Brazil;
Baron Descamps, Senator, Belgian Minister of State;
M. Francis Hagerup, Envoy Extraordinary and Minister Plenipotentiary of His Majesty the King of Norway, at Stockholm;
M. Albert de Lapradelle, Professor of the Faculty of Law of the University of Paris;
Dr. Loder, Member of the Supreme Court of the Netherlands;
Lord Phillimore, Member of the Privy Council of His Majesty the King of England;
M. Arturo Ricci-Busatti, Minister Plenipotentiary of His Majesty the King of Italy, Legal Adviser to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Italy;
Mr. Elihu Root, Former Secretary of State of the United States.
These gentlemen held their sessions at the Hague from June 16, to July 24, 1920, and as a result of their labours, produced the project or plan of the constitution or statute establishing such a court, which statute, having been submitted first to the Council of the League, and by the council transmitted to the Assembly of the League for consideration, and having, in passing through the council and through the assembly, been subjected to certain amendments, with which amendments it was unanimously assented to by the Assembly, is the statute that is now before us.

The minister should
frankly state why this money was not spent then. If he will tell us why, he will see the most extraordinary spectacle of the loyal Opposition helping him to keep his supporters from squandering the public money. It is true that the member for Annapolis is an active member of the Government. I do not wish to lecture him;
I was told before that I had no right to do so; but notwithstanding what his position in the Tory party of Canada may be to-day, I say that he has no right to force an honest and respectable man like the Minister of Public Works to spend even $5 of the people's money unless such expenditure is absolutely fair and just. So long as the minister does not give an explanation of these matters we shall be justified in opposing this item.
